WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.145
Idea. You have ten pounds of pocket money. A cinema trip costs ten pounds, and so does a new phone case. You want both, but you can only afford one.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:26.182
Countries face the same problem. People's wants never run out, but resources, land, labour, capital and enterprise, are limited. This is scarcity. On this curve, point Z is wanted but cannot be made with the resources available.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:36.486
Needs are things we must have to survive: food, water and shelter. Wants are the extras we would like, such as holidays, phones and education.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:47.095
When you choose, you give something up. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative given up. Choose the cinema, and the opportunity cost is the phone case.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:58.559
Three economic agents make choices. A consumer buys for their own use. A producer makes and supplies goods and services. A government runs the country.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:09.660
Method. Step one, start from scarcity: the agent has limited money. Step two, say who chose and what they chose, using names from the question.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:22.655
Step three, name one realistic alternative. Step four, explain that the budget cannot pay for both, so it is given up. A family, a firm or a government all work the same way.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:38.009
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2024. Casper has decided to buy a new television. Explain one possible opportunity cost for Casper of this decision. Three marks.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.339
First, name one realistic alternative. Casper might also have wanted a new laptop.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:58.913
Then explain. Casper's money is limited, so he cannot afford both. The laptop was his next best choice, so he sacrifices it. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:08.635
Common mistake. A common mistake is saying the opportunity cost is the money paid. It is the next best thing you could have had instead.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.083
Another is listing lots of things. Name one single next best alternative.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:29.017
Your turn. Now you try. A government has enough money to build a new hospital or a new road, but not both. It builds the hospital. Explain one possible opportunity cost. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.471
Answer. The government chose the hospital, so the alternative given up is the new road.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:44.775
Its budget is limited, so it cannot pay for both. The road was the next best choice, so drivers lose the faster journeys it would have given.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:52.993
Remember: who chose what, one next best alternative, and why both cannot be had. Now try the practice questions.
